\section{Separate Value Identity from Register Identity}

At a low level, an instruction may implicitly read a flag, write a fixed result register, or clobber caller-saved registers. Assigning an SSA name to the result version identifies which value a use needs. It does not excuse the compiler from satisfying the instruction's concrete operand constraints. Allen Leung and Lal George's \emph{Static Single Assignment Form for Machine Code} separates renaming for optimization from reconstruction of references to dedicated physical registers. When a later instruction still needs an earlier value of a dedicated register, compensation copies can preserve the value without pretending the machine has two versions of that register.

Consider a selected-IR fragment where \texttt{RET} is a physical return register, \texttt{keep} is an unconstrained virtual location, and \texttt{g} clobbers \texttt{RET}. The SSA names \texttt{v1} and \texttt{v2} represent different values:

\begin{verbatim}
v1 = call f()       # result required in RET
v2 = call g()       # result required in RET
return sub(v1, v2)
\end{verbatim}

Mapping both values directly to \texttt{RET} makes the subtraction read the result of \texttt{g} twice. The emitted code needs to save \texttt{v1} before the second call, to a location that survives that call, and read it from there. \texttt{keep} can ultimately be a suitably saved register or a spill slot; an arbitrary other caller-saved register is not enough. Return the result in the required ABI location. A mock target test with \texttt{f} returning \texttt{9} and \texttt{g} returning \texttt{4} should return \texttt{5}, not \texttt{0}. Also test a use of a flag across a flag-writing instruction and overlapping registers if the target has them.

\section{Direct SSA Interpretation Needs Edge State}

A VM can retain value names as instruction-result slots instead of deciding on physical registers ahead of time. In \emph{A Virtual Machine for Interpreting Programs in Static Single Assignment Form}, Jeffery von Ronne, Ning Wang, Alexander Apel, and Michael Franz describe an interpretable SSA form with a control-flow edge number set by branching instructions. Each \texttt{phi} reads the operand for that number. Knowing the destination program counter is not always enough; two edges into the same block can supply different operands even when they come from the same terminator.

Static single definition does not mean that a result slot is only written once per activation. A loop revisits the same instruction slots. Two loop merges can read each other's previous values; immediate writes would make later merges see the new state. The paper buffers the transfers and commits them at an explicit end-of-merge instruction. The interpreter's contract is that all merge operands are read using the incoming state and all ordinary instructions see the committed state. The swap from Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-207} is a direct regression: run zero, one, and two iterations and check that the pair alternates, including when it is not the first merge in a larger group.

