\section{Dispatch Parallel Edges by Identity}

An SSA interpreter that records only where a jump came from and
where it landed can still choose the wrong merge operand. Some IRs
allow distinct successor slots from one terminator to target the same
block. For a verified IR of that kind, let an edge identity include
the source and successor slot. Assume that the terminator has
evaluated its condition and that each merge maps every legal incoming
edge to a readable operand. Transfer control using the selected slot
as well as the destination. The following routine covers only block
entry; ordinary instructions run after its writes are committed.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Source $B$, selected successor slot $k$, frame $F$
\Let{e}{\operatorname{edge}(B,k)}
\Let{J}{\operatorname{target}(e)}
\Let{W}{[\,]}
\ForAll{merges $m$ at entry of $J$}
  \To{Read the selected edge, not just the source block.}
  \Let{s}{\operatorname{operand}(m,e)}
  \State Append $(\operatorname{slot}(m),\operatorname{value}(F,s))$ to $W$
\EndFor
\To{No merge sees a result written in this group.}
\ForAll{$(d,v)\in W$}
  \Let{F[d]}{v}
\EndFor
\State Continue at the first ordinary instruction of $J$
\end{sudocode}

Let entry choose slot $0$ when the flag is true, and slot $1$
otherwise. Both slots jump to $J$. At $J$, a merge picks $12$
on slot $0$ and $47$ on slot $1$; the next instruction adds $3$
and returns. True must yield $15$, false must yield $50$.
A dispatch table keyed by predecessor block alone can store only
one of $12$ and $47$, making at least one run wrong. Splitting the
two edges into distinct intermediate blocks is a useful independent
test: the transformed procedure should produce the same two results.

The buffer contains only values from the incoming frame, so both a
parallel-edge diamond and a loop-carried swap have the specified
simultaneous semantics. Assuming direct indexing, a transfer into a
block with $m$ merges costs $O(m)$ time and up to $m$ scratch entries.
In an IR that forbids parallel edges, split them before forming the
merge table; silently collapsing them changes semantics. A serialized
interpreter format should check that a successor slot actually points
to the block whose table will be indexed.

\section{Choose the Boundary Where Constraints Become Explicit}

Direct interpretation avoids lowering merges into ordinary moves before execution, but it adds branch metadata and merge handling to the dispatcher. The verifier should check edge indices, matching operand counts, and legal entry points into a merge group. A stack-bytecode VM has different costs: on translation into SSA, incoming bytecode stack shapes and local types must be reconciled at joins, and values from each incoming frame state can become arguments to a block. Neither execution format implies that the VM must use that same format for its optimizer.

For a tiered runtime, record where each logical frame value lives at a safepoint or how to reconstruct it. A native frame might keep a value in a saved register, in a spill slot, or only as a rematerialization recipe. Reaching a native instruction with all registers ``correct'' for the upcoming arithmetic is insufficient if the runtime cannot rebuild the interpreter frame there. The dossier's Chapters~2 and~4, \emph{Multi-Level Interpreter} and \emph{Deoptimization as an IR Operation}, expand this state correspondence into an interface between tiers.
